    "result_text": "All 33 tests pass with a green build. \n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the bulk-signup warning so that a created owner's response carries `bulkSignupWarning: true` once more than 80 owners already exist for that registration day, and `false` otherwise.\n\nFollowing the existing `namesakeCount` pattern (a creation-time snapshot persisted on the owner and echoed back via `GET /api/owners/{id}`):\n\n- **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 captured the daily count once (`ownersRegisteredToday = countOwnersRegisteredOn(registrationDate)`), reused it for the existing `>= 100` daily-limit check, and set `owner.setBulkSignupWarning(ownersRegisteredToday > 80)`. This reuses the exact same accumulation path as the daily-limit rule, as the test's comment describes.\n- **`Owner` model** \u2014 added a `bulkSignupWarning` field (`@Column(name = \"bulk_signup_warning\")`) with getter/setter. MapStruct maps it to the DTO by name automatically.\n- **Schema** \u2014 added a `bulk_signup_warning BOOLEAN` column to all four DB schemas (hsqldb \u2014 used by tests with `ddl-auto=none`, plus h2, mysql, postgres for consistency).\n- **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the `bulkSignupWarning` boolean property to the `Owner` response schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` includes it.\n\nThe flag is set on every create, so a fresh owner reports `false` (Cp22's asserted branch), and it flips to `true` once the day's tally exceeds 80.",
